The problem asks us to simulate a process involving $N$ aliens.
There are $N$ aliens, indexed $1$ to $N$.
The $i$-th alien starts with $A_i$ stones.
The $i$-th alien becomes an adult exactly $i$ years later.
When an alien becomes an adult, every *current* adult who has at least one stone gives exactly one stone to the newly adult alien.
We need to find the number of stones each alien has after $N$ years.
The constraints are $N \le 5 \times 10^5$ and $A_i \le 5 \times 10^5$.

Let's trace the process year by year.
Year 0: Initial state. Aliens $1, \dots, N$ have $A_1, \dots, A_N$ stones.
At year $i$ (where $i$ goes from 1 to $N$), the $i$-th alien becomes an adult.
Wait, the problem says "will become an adult exactly $i$ years later".
So at year 1, alien 1 becomes an adult.
At year 2, alien 2 becomes an adult.
...
At year $N$, alien $N$ becomes an adult.

Let's clarify the condition "every adult who has at least one stone".
Who are the adults?
Initially, no one is an adult.
At year 1, alien 1 becomes an adult. Before this event, no one is an adult. So no one gives a stone.
Wait, let's re-read carefully.
"When someone becomes an adult on this planet, every adult who has at least one stone gives exactly one stone as a congratulatory gift to the alien who has just become an adult."
This implies that the set of adults grows.
Let $S_t$ be the set of aliens who are adults at year $t$.
At year $i$, alien $i$ becomes an adult.
So, for $t < i$, alien $i$ is not an adult.
For $t \ge i$, alien $i$ is an adult.
Specifically, at year $i$, just before the event, aliens $1, 2, \dots, i-1$ are adults. Alien $i$ is about to become an adult.
So the set of adults giving gifts is $\{1, 2, \dots, i-1\}$.
Wait, does alien $i$ give a stone to themselves? No, "to the alien who has just become an adult".
So alien $i$ receives stones from aliens $1, \dots, i-1$ who are currently adults and have $\ge 1$ stone.
After receiving stones, alien $i$ becomes an adult.
Then at year $i+1$, aliens $1, \dots, i$ are adults. Alien $i+1$ becomes an adult.
Aliens $1, \dots, i$ give stones to alien $i+1$ if they have $\ge 1$ stone.

Let's check the sample 1 to verify this understanding.
